                        "entry_id": "c2793574-f59b-3ac1-906e-5808b3dcbb82",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The ritual slaughter of a bull."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Ancient-Rome",
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "47b8936b-edb3-4547-9389-6b1c19064de9",
                                "sense_id": "5db24588-df7c-3fdf-b37b-fdaf1e06339c",
                                "text": "The cult of the Magna Mater, borrowing the civic spirit proper to Roman religion, made of the official taurobolium a manifestation of loyalty to the institutions of the Roman Empire."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "abaaaa2a-d57e-4550-810b-3a8d2a74b7bc",
                                "sense_id": "5db24588-df7c-3fdf-b37b-fdaf1e06339c",
                                "text": "1968, Phoenix, Volume 22, Classical Association of Canada, University of Toronto Press, page 236,\nIt is safe to conclude that the taurobolium at this stage in its development was a substitution rite³³ at least in its original and most basic purpose, although it may soon have become an initiatory rite for women."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "090e4598-0664-482a-a8e6-bceac5efea8d",
                                "sense_id": "5db24588-df7c-3fdf-b37b-fdaf1e06339c",
                                "text": "1990, Britt-Mari Näsström, O Mother of the Gods and Men, Plus Ultra, page 42,\nIn addition to the gloomy figure of Attis, another spectacular element was connected to the cult of Magna Mater, the rite of the Taurobolium and the Criobolium."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
